why 2 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The chord over the bass's G is really iv⁷ (G–B♭–D–F), reached as the bass leaps D–G. The checker hears the B♭ chord held over the bar line; the bass's move makes it a new harmony.
why 2 1/8 LEAPS The tenor falls E–D and skips to B♭. Its ends form a tritone, but the line moves mostly by step through two chords.
why 5 3/4 LEAPS The tenor climbs from B to F through the bar, mostly by step. Its ends form a tritone, but no single leap spans it.
why 6 1/8 LEAPS After its diminished leap down to B the tenor turns back up by a fourth to E, inside the leap. It returns inward, by a fourth instead of a step.
why 6 1/4 SPACING The bass drops an octave to a low E for the dominant of the half cadence while the tenor stays in its middle range. The deep bass gives the cadence weight.
why 6 3/8 SPACING The same wide gap; at the fermata the bass moves to A and the gap narrows.
why 7 1/2 LOW_SPACING The bass climbs A–B–C♯ under the tenor's E–D, so for one beat they sit a third apart low down; then the tenor leaps up to A for V⁶.
why 8 1/4 RANGE The bass drops to a deep D under the held D minor chord, preparing the half cadence on A.
